<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mogadishu (1993)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-civil-war">Somali Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mogadishu was fought on 3–4 October 1993 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> capital, when US special forces trying to capture lieutenants of the warlord Mohamed Farrah Aidid were trapped after two Black Hawk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopters</a> were shot down. Eighteen American soldiers and hundreds of Somalis were killed. The battle led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and made Western powers wary of intervening in African conflicts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mogadishu-1993.jpg" alt="Battle of Mogadishu (1993)" width="300" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Hawk_Down_Super64_over_Mogadishu_coast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> sent forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> to relieve the famine of 1992, which followed the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>'s regime in early 1991 and a severe drought. In March 1993 the Security Council authorised a second mission, UNOSOM II, to establish a secure environment and promote national reconciliation. On 5 June 1993 fighters of the Somali National Alliance (SNA) and civilians attacked Pakistani troops inspecting an arms cache at Radio Mogadishu, and the UN blamed the SNA leader Mohamed Farah Aidid.</p><p>On 12 July, in the raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a> call Bloody Monday, US helicopters attacked a house where clan elders were meeting. UNOSOM said it had struck an SNA command centre, an account disputed by Somalis, journalists and human rights groups. After bombs killed American soldiers in August, President Bill Clinton sent Task Force Ranger, including 400 Rangers and Delta Force operators, to capture Aidid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 3 October a local informant told the CIA that two of Aidid's advisors, Omar Salad Elmi and Abdi Hassan Awale, would meet near the Olympic Hotel in the Bakara market area. At 13:50 sixteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopters</a> took off from the airport for the target. About 40 minutes into the assault a rocket-propelled grenade struck the Black Hawk Super 61, which crashed about 300 yards from the target, killing both pilots.</p><p>At about 16:40 another grenade hit the tail rotor of Super 64, piloted by Michael Durant, which crashed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> shacks. Some 90 Rangers and Delta Force soldiers were trapped overnight around the first crash site, fighting at close quarters and occupying four houses.</p><p>A first relief convoy led by Lieutenant Colonel Lawrence Casper turned back under heavy fire at the KM4 junction. A larger UNOSOM II convoy of Pakistani, Malaysian and American troops pushed into the city and rescued the survivors the next morning, taking further casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Eighteen American soldiers were killed and 73 wounded, one Malaysian soldier was killed and seven wounded, and two Pakistani troops were injured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> dead, a mixture of fighters and civilians, are variously estimated at between 133 and 700; the Red Cross estimated 200, and Aidid claimed 315. Crowds dragged the bodies of several American dead through the streets, an act shown on American television. A captain of the SNA said it was done by enraged civilians who had lost relatives, and pointed to Bloody Monday.</p><p>On 6 October Clinton ordered a halt to all US action against Aidid except in self-defence and announced that US forces would leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> by 31 March 1994. The last UN forces withdrew by early 1995. The wariness of large interventions that followed is often called the Somalia Syndrome. Mark Bowden's book Black Hawk Down appeared in 1999, and Ridley Scott's film of it in 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Mogadishu (1993)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rival factions fought over Mogadishu, and famine in 1992 killed hundreds of thousands, prompting UN and US intervention, which ended after the <b>Battle of Mogadishu</b> in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a></li></ul></section>
